  "passages": [
    {
      "title": "The thesis",
      "role": "Core thesis",
      "excerpt": "We do not receive a short life, but we make it a short one, and we are not poor in days, but wasteful of them.",
      "note": "This is the central claim of the essay. Seneca is not mainly complaining that death comes too soon; he is saying life feels short because people hand it over without judgment. Read every later example as evidence for this ownership problem: money, status, work, pleasure, and public duty can all spend the same life."
    },
    {
      "title": "False necessity",
      "role": "Important principle",
      "excerpt": "Life is long enough, if you know how to use it.",
      "note": "Important because it attacks the excuse that the life problem is only external. Seneca does not deny real obligations, but he keeps forcing the reader to separate necessity from imitation, fear, approval, and drift. The useful test is whether a supposedly unavoidable demand was chosen by judgment or copied from pressure."
    },
    {
      "title": "No one belongs to himself",
      "role": "Core diagnosis",
      "excerpt": "A is devoted to B, and B to C: no one belongs to himself.",
      "note": "This is the cleanest diagnosis of the outsourcing-of-life problem. A day can be full, respected, and socially useful while still not belonging to the person living it. The point is not isolation; it is whether your attention and choices have an owner."
    },
    {
      "title": "Property versus time",
      "role": "Supportive analogy",
      "excerpt": "You cannot find any one who wants to distribute his money; yet among how many people does every one distribute his life?",
      "note": "Supportive analogy: Seneca makes time feel like property because people already understand defending property. The analogy supports the thesis by making attention loss concrete. It is especially relevant if you are generous with objects but careless with tired phone-drift time."
    },
    {
      "title": "Augustus wanted out",
      "role": "Supportive historical example",
      "excerpt": "For this reason he longed for leisure: all his labours were based upon hopes and thoughts of leisure.",
      "note": "Supportive historical example: Augustus is the strongest status case Seneca can choose. If the first emperor, with maximum power and honor, still dreams of being released from the role, then status cannot be the cure for the time problem. The example supports the broader trend: identity plus responsibility can become a prison even when everyone else calls it success."
    },
    {
      "title": "Half a prisoner",
      "role": "Supportive historical example",
      "excerpt": "I am living in my Tusculan villa almost as a prisoner.",
      "note": "Supportive historical example: Cicero has the retreat, the villa, and the distance from public life, but not inner freedom. Seneca uses him to show that leisure is not a location. You can leave the office, city, or group and still be mentally owned by responsibility, identity, politics, and reputation."
    },
    {
      "title": "Postponed life",
      "role": "Core practical warning",
      "excerpt": "There is no such obstacle to true living as waiting, which loses to-day while it is depending on the morrow.",
      "note": "This is one of the most practical lines in the whole essay. It is not anti-planning; it is anti-postponement. Seneca is warning against storing real life inside an ideal future identity while the usable day remains unchosen."
    },
    {
      "title": "The first day flies",
      "role": "Important compressed quote",
      "excerpt": "The best of unhappy mortals’ days is the first to fly.",
      "note": "Important because the poetry is easy to misread. It does not mean childhood or youth is automatically best. It means the nearest usable day is the best one because it is the first to disappear; if you wait for the ideal season, the actual day has already been spent."
    },
    {
      "title": "Learning how to live",
      "role": "Core reframing",
      "excerpt": "One's whole life must be spent in learning how to live, and, which may perhaps surprise you more, one's whole life must be spent in learning how to die.",
      "note": "This reframes the essay away from productivity. Seneca is not saying to optimize every hour into output; he is saying living itself is a discipline. Learning how to die means keeping mortality in view so your choices are cleaner while you are alive."
    }
  ],
