      "readerEdit": [
        "Most people, Paulinus, complain that nature has treated us badly because we are born for such a short time. They say that the span given to us runs off so quickly, so violently, that almost everyone reaches the end just when he is getting ready to enjoy life. This complaint is not only made by the ordinary crowd or by thoughtless people. Even great men have said the same thing. That is where the familiar saying of physicians comes from: art is long, but life is short. Aristotle himself, in a way not quite fitting for a wise man, quarreled with nature because animals can live ten or fifteen centuries, while man, born for so many great tasks, has his existence cut so much shorter. But this is the mistake: we are not given too little time; we lose a great deal of it. Life is long enough for the highest work if the whole of it is arranged well. But when it is wasted in luxury and carelessness, when it is not directed toward any good purpose, then only at the end are we forced to feel that it is over, even though we never noticed it slipping away. So the truth is this: we do not receive a short life; we make it short. We are not poor in days; we are wasteful of them. Great royal wealth disappears quickly when it falls into the hands of a bad owner, while even a modest fortune grows when it is entrusted to a wise guardian. In the same way, life is large enough for the person who knows how to use it."
      ],
