      "readerEdit": [
        "Why do we blame nature? She has treated us kindly. Life is long enough if you know how to use it. One man is ruled by greed that nothing can satisfy. Another wears himself out with effort spent on work that is completely useless. Another is soaked in wine. Another is numb with idleness. One man is exhausted by an ambition that depends on the approval of other people.[2] Another, restless as a merchant, is driven over every land and sea by the hope of profit. Some are driven by military desire and spend their lives either putting other people in danger or trembling for themselves. Some wear away their lives in voluntary slavery, serving powerful men without reward. Many are busy trying to claim someone else's fortune or complaining about their own. A large number have no settled purpose at all; they are thrown from one plan to another by a wandering, inconsistent, dissatisfied mind. Some care about nothing strongly enough to try for it, and simply lie there yawning until fate comes for them. So I do not doubt the truth of the poet's line, spoken as if it were an oracle:",
        "\"We live a small part only of our lives.\"",
        "The rest of our duration is time, but not life. Vices press on us from every side. They do not let us stand up, raise our eyes, and look at truth. They keep us down, pinned to low desires. People in this condition are never allowed to return to themselves. If they ever happen to get a little rest, they still roll back and forth like the deep sea after a storm; even when the wind has stopped, the water keeps moving. Their desires never give them peace. Do you think I am speaking only about people whose ruin is obvious? Look instead at the people whose success everyone runs to admire. They are choked by their own good fortune. How many are weighed down by riches? How many have been destroyed by their eloquence and constant need to show their cleverness?[3] How many are pale from constant pleasure? How many have no freedom left because a crowd of clients surrounds them? Look from the lowest to the highest. This man calls friends to support him, this one appears in court, this one is accused, this one pleads, this one sits on the jury. But no one claims himself; everyone spends himself on someone else. Examine the famous names, the people everyone talks about, and you will see the same pattern: A is devoted to B, and B to C; no one belongs to himself. Some people are even angry in the most unreasonable way. They complain that their superiors did not receive them when they wanted an audience. But what right does a man have to complain that someone else shut him out, when he never has time to give his own conscience a hearing? That powerful man, however arrogant, will one day look at you, listen to you, and give you a seat beside him. But you never arrange to look at yourself or listen to your own case. You should not demand this attention from another person when, even while asking for it, you were not trying to meet someone else so much as failing to meet yourself.[4]"
      ],
