      "readerEditNotes": [
        "Key line: \"Life is long enough, if you know how to use it.\" The list of greed, useless work, drink, idleness, ambition, trade, soldiering, law, clients, and approval is not a random vice list. It is Seneca showing many different ways a life can stop belonging to itself.",
        "Names/context: \"Clients\" are not customers. In Roman patronage, clients depended on powerful patrons for access, favors, money, protection, or status, and repaid them with attendance, loyalty, and public support. That is why Seneca can call respectable public life a kind of voluntary slavery.",
        "Present-day application: appetite, approval, ambition, and respectable busyness can all claim a life. The category matters less than whether the activity was chosen deliberately and still serves a purpose worth its cost."
      ]
