      "readerEdit": [
        "If all the sharpest minds in history gathered around this one subject, they still could not fully express how strange this blindness is. People will not let anyone settle on their land. If there is even a tiny dispute about a boundary line, they run to stones and clubs. Yet they let others walk right into their lives, and they often invite the takers in themselves. You cannot find anyone who wants to hand out his money freely, yet almost everyone hands out his life. People guard property carefully, but when it comes to time, the one thing they should guard most, they are wildly wasteful. Take an old man and say to him: \"You have reached the farthest edge of human life. You are a hundred, or even older. Now give an honest account of your whole life. Tell us how much time went to creditors, how much to a mistress, how much to a king, how much to clients, how much to fighting with your wife, how much to controlling slaves, how much to running all over the city on business. Add the illnesses we cause by our own habits, and the time that lay unused. You will see that you have not lived as many years as you count. Look back and ask how often you were steady in your purpose, how many days passed exactly as you intended, how often you were in command of yourself, how much work you really completed, how many people stole parts of your life without your noticing, how much was taken by useless grief, foolish joy, greedy desire, or polite conversation. See how little of yourself is left. Then you will understand that you are dying before your time.\" Why does this happen? Because people live as if they will live forever. You do not remember how fragile you are. You do not notice how much time has already gone. You spend a day on this person or that thing as if your supply were full and overflowing, when that day may be your last. You fear everything like a mortal, but desire everything like an immortal. You will hear many people say, \"After my fiftieth year I will give myself to leisure. My sixtieth year will be my last year in public office.\" But what guarantee do you have that life will last that long? Who will let everything unfold exactly as you arranged it? Are you not ashamed to save only the leftovers of life for yourself, and to set aside for a sound mind only the time that no business can use? How late it is to begin living when you are already near leaving. What foolish forgetfulness of death, to postpone good sense until fifty or sixty, and to choose to begin life at an age that few people even reach."
      ],
