        "Key line: people defend property, boundaries, and money, but \"distribute\" their lives. Seneca wants the contrast to feel absurd: you would notice someone taking a device or bank balance, but you might not notice whole evenings leaking into passive attention.",
        "Names/context: estates, creditors, mistresses, kings, clients, slaves, and city business are Roman account-book categories. Seneca turns the life into an audit: who got the hours, what did they buy with them, and how much of the person is left?",
        "Present-day application: generosity is not the problem. The sharper question is whether low-energy time has a restorative default that still belongs to the person living it, rather than disappearing by accident."
