        "Key line: Livius Drusus says he had no holidays even as a boy. Seneca is not only mocking ambition; he is warning that serious public responsibility can become a lifelong urgency loop.",
        "Names/context: Drusus was a Roman reformer tied to dangerous conflicts over law, citizenship, and Italian allies. The Gracchi were earlier reform brothers whose politics became symbols of high-stakes reform and social conflict. The praetexta was a bordered toga worn by boys and officials, so Seneca is emphasizing how young Drusus was.",
        "Present-day application: good motives do not automatically create a coherent life. Important-looking motion still needs a chosen direction, a stopping rule, and evidence that it is becoming something."
