      "readerEdit": [
        "Among the busy, I count first those who give their time to nothing but drinking and dissipation. No one is busy in a more shameful way. Others may chase a false glory, but at least there is some show of effort in it. You can name misers, angry men, haters, and people who fight wars without cause; such people at least sin like human beings. But the people given over to gluttony and lust have a more disgraceful sickness. Examine all the hours of their lives. Count how much time they spend calculating, plotting, fearing, flattering and being flattered, entering legal pledges for themselves or others, and arranging banquets that have become serious business. You will see that neither their pleasures nor their pains give them breathing room. Everyone agrees that nothing serious, whether eloquence or literature, can be done well by a mind occupied elsewhere. Nothing takes deep root in a mind pointed at another subject, a mind that rejects whatever you try to plant in it. No one understands living less than a man of constant business; nothing is harder to learn. Other arts have teachers everywhere. Some arts can be learned so thoroughly by boys that they can teach them to others. But a whole life must be spent learning how to live, and, which may surprise you more, a whole life must be spent learning how to die. Many excellent men have freed themselves from obstacles, given up riches, business, and pleasure, and made it their task until the end to learn how to live; yet most of them leave life admitting that they still do not know how to live, much less how to live wisely. Believe me: it takes a great person, someone above ordinary weakness, not to allow any part of his time to be stolen. Such a life is very long because every possible part is devoted to itself. No part lies idle or uncultivated; no part is under another person's control. He finds nothing worth trading for time, so he guards time with the greatest care. He has enough time. Those who surrender much of their lives to people and necessity do not. Still, do not think they never notice the loss. You will hear many who are troubled by great prosperity cry out among clients, court cases, and honorable burdens, \"I am not allowed to live my own life.\" Why not? Because everyone who calls you to defend him takes you away from yourself. How many days were spent by that defendant, that candidate for office, that old woman tired of burying heirs, that man pretending to be sick so inheritance-hunters will care about him, that powerful friend who uses you as decoration instead of treating you as a friend? Balance your account. Run through all the days of your life. You will see that only a few days, and only days useless for other people's purposes, were left to you. A man who obtains the fasces, the office-symbols he once wanted, immediately wants to be rid of them and says, \"When will this year be over?\" Another gives public games; once he would have paid heavily for the chance, but now he says, \"When will I escape this?\" Another is a lawyer praised throughout the courts, drawing crowds that fill the forum farther than they can hear him, and he says, \"When will vacation come?\" Everyone hurries through life, tired of the present and reaching for the future. But the person who arranges all his time for his own purposes, who orders each day as part of his life, does not wish for tomorrow or fear it. What new pleasure could another hour bring him? He knows pleasures already and has had enough of them. Fortune may handle the rest as she likes; his life is already safe from her. Such a man may gain something, but cannot lose anything. Any gain comes like extra food to a person already full: he may take it, but he does not need it. So you should not think a man has lived long just because he has gray hair and wrinkles. He has not lived long; he has merely existed for a long time. Would you think a man had made a long voyage if a storm caught him as soon as he left port, drove him in circles, and different winds kept throwing him around the same place? He has not traveled much. He has only been tossed around."
      ],
