      "readerEdit": [
        "The only people truly at leisure are those who devote themselves to philosophy. They alone really live, because they do not merely enjoy their own lifetime; they add every century to it. All the years before them belong to them. Unless we are utterly ungrateful, we will regard the noblest minds, the founders of the great schools of thought, as having been born for us and as having prepared life for us. By the labor of others we are led to see beautiful things brought out of darkness into light. We are not shut out from any age. We can enter every subject. If only we have strength of mind to step beyond the narrow limit of human weakness, we have a vast stretch of time in which to move. We may argue with Socrates, doubt with Carneades, rest with Epicurus, overcome human nature with the Stoics, and go beyond ordinary human limits with the Cynics. Since nature allows us to commune with every age, why do we not pull ourselves away from our own petty, fleeting span and give ourselves wholly to what is vast, eternal, and shared with better people than ourselves? Those who run around in a cycle of calls, troubling themselves and others, may indulge their madness fully. They may cross every patron's threshold every day, leave no open door unentered, and carry their self-interested greetings through houses of every kind. But after all, how few people can they see in such a large city divided among so many different desires? How many will deny them admission through idleness, self-indulgence, or rudeness? How many, after making them wait, will rush past with a fake hurry? How many will avoid coming through the entrance hall crowded with clients and escape by a hidden back door, as if deceiving the visitor were not ruder than refusing him? How many, half asleep and stupid from yesterday's drinking, can barely return the greeting of the poor man who broke his own rest to wait on theirs, even after his name has been whispered for the thousandth time, except with an offensive yawn from half-open lips? We may truly say that those are following the real path of duty who wish every day to spend familiar time with Zeno, Pythagoras, Democritus, the other high priests of virtue, and with Aristotle and Theophrastus. None of these men will be \"busy.\" None will fail to send you away happier and on better terms with yourself. None will let you leave empty-handed. Their company can be enjoyed by everyone, by night as well as by day."
      ],
