      "readerEdit": [
        "None of these teachers will force you to die, but all of them will teach you how to die. None will waste your time; each adds his own time to yours. Their talk is not dangerous. Their friendship will not lead you to execution. Their company will not ruin you with expenses. You may take whatever you want from them, and they will not stop you from drinking as deeply of their wisdom as you please. What happiness, what a good old age, awaits the person who takes these people as patrons. He will have friends with whom he can discuss great and small matters, whose advice he can ask daily about himself, from whom he will hear truth without insult and praise without flattery, and after whose likeness he can shape his own character. We often say that we cannot choose our parents because they were assigned by chance. Yet we can be born as we choose: there are many families of the noblest minds. Choose which family you want to belong to. By adoption you will receive not only their name, but also their property. This property is not to be guarded in a small or miserly spirit; the more people you share it with, the larger it becomes. These teachers will open the path to eternity and raise you to a height from which no one can cast you down. By this means alone you can prolong mortal life, even turn it into something immortal. High office, monuments, decrees of ambition, stones piled in honor, all quickly pass away. Time casts down and ruins everything. But what philosophy has sealed cannot be injured. No age will discard it or lessen its force; each later century adds something to the respect in which it is held. We look jealously at what is near us, but admire what is far away with less prejudice. Therefore the wise man's life includes much. He is not fenced in by the same limits as others. He alone is free from the laws that govern mankind. All ages serve him like a god. If time is past, he recalls it by memory. If it is present, he uses it. If it is future, he anticipates it. His life is long because he gathers all times into one."
      ],
